   "name": "The action of electrical energy on materials"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "When an electric current passes through a material it can produce:\n• a heating effect: the wire gets hot (electric iron, hot plate, kettle, water heater, fuse);\n• a lighting effect: a thin wire glows (filament bulb), or a lamp gives light (LED, fluorescent tube);\n• a magnetic effect: a coil of wire with current becomes a magnet (electromagnet, electric bell, motor, loudspeaker);\n• a chemical effect: current breaks down some liquids (electrolysis); it is used for electroplating and for charging batteries.\nA fuse is a thin wire that melts if the current is too big, to protect the circuit.\nElectrical energy is converted into other forms: heat, light, movement, sound.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "d": "an electric kettle"
  },
  "problem": "Which effect of electric current does {d} use, and what energy change happens?",
  "steps": [
   "The current passes through a heating element, which gets very hot: the heating effect.",
   "Electrical energy is changed into heat energy, which boils the water."
  ],
  "answer": "The heating effect: electrical energy → heat.",
  "checks": []
 },
